Patch of ST-JEPA v2 (sebastianbirk/stjepa, b433d5e) for the OVCA ablations and the mouse reruns.
Apply with: git apply stjepa_ovca_ablation.patch (setup_code.sh does this). Changes that alter results are opt-in or
limited to code paths that were broken. With the default config keys and arguments, tokenized data and training are
unchanged, except the `gt_type: rank` metatoken if the token dictionary's offset differs from the hard-coded one (8);
so is the default inference (metatoken removed), except cell embeddings of cell-neighborhood models (10).

Training
 1. Opt-in seed: a top-level 'seed' key replaces the hard-coded _GLOBAL_SEED = 0 for numpy/torch seeding and the
    length-grouped sampler (and thereby the DataLoader worker seeds: masks and gene sampling).
 2. stjepa-latest.pth.tar is written to a temporary file and renamed, so a kill during saving keeps the previous
    checkpoint instead of leaving a truncated file that silently restarts training.
 3. Predictor in batches whose special tokens are padded (mask.special_token_pad_ratio > 0): the new_spc layout put
    the first context gene in the first target's slot; such batches now use the plain concatenation. Batches with
    special tokens are unchanged.
 4. Predictor segment table: one special segment per special token, as in the encoder (crashed with >= 2).
 5. restrict_special_attention: a padded special token attends to itself instead of to nothing (NaN outputs that
    spread to all tokens without flash attention).
 6. Opt-in mask.pad_masks_to_batch_max: context and target masks are padded to the longest in the batch (index 0,
    marked invalid) instead of trimmed to the shortest, so every cell keeps all its sampled context and target tokens;
    the context encoder and the predictor ignore padded entries as keys and the loss averages over real target tokens.
    With equally long masks, training is bit-identical to trimming.

Datasets
 7. Dataset-level special token padding: one pad token per special token (was n_special_tokens per special token),
    and padded `gt_type: rank` special tokens stay 0.
 8. `gt_type: rank` special tokens use the special value token offset of the token dictionary
    (spv_start - 2 - n_cls) instead of the offset hard-coded by vocabulary size; a warning is printed if they differ.

Inference
 9. keep_special_tokens (embed_dataset, harmonize_tokenize_embed_pipeline, gene_embed_dataset, infer): keep the batch
    metatoken as in training. Default False: removed, as before.
10. Inference reads restrict_special_attention from `mask` like training (fallback `meta`), passes data.nz_spc to the
    dataset, gene_embed_dataset uses data.n_special_values like training, and the cell-only context uses
    data.seq_len_cell (was (seq_len - n_special_tokens) // n_segments, which gave wrong cell embeddings for
    cell-neighborhood models; neighborhood embeddings are unchanged).
11. tokenize_adata (re-tokenization from a model folder) passes data.seq_len_cell for cell-neighborhood models and the
    tokenizer options recorded in the model config (absent keys: off).

Tokenizers (opt-in constructor arguments, default off = output unchanged)
12. stable_rank_ties: genes are ordered by rank-stream score (descending), count-stream value (descending), reference
    nonzero mean (ascending; 'nonzero_mean' of the norm factor file) and token id (ascending), independently of gene
    order and sort algorithm, in all paths and both cell-neighborhood blocks.
13. zero_expr_from_raw_counts: the candidate genes of a cell (neighborhood) are the genes with raw (aggregated) count
    > 0, whatever their normalized score (Seurat v3 / Pearson residuals no longer select undetected genes).
    With 12 or 13, ranked gene lists are cut at model_input_size (lossless; 6-14x less memory on the dense path).
14. exact_n_nonzero_tokens: n_nonzero_tokens (read only by the length-grouped sampler) is the number of gene tokens
    of the sequence; it was the number of ranked-list entries, which on the dense path (rank and count normalized
    differently) includes pad entries, so every cell had the full length and batches were not grouped by size. With
    12 or 13, the dense path also drops trailing pad entries of the ranked lists (lossless; about 2x less memory).
15. seurat_loess_span (also normalize_by_seurat(loess_span=...)): the Seurat v3 LOESS fit of log variance on log mean
    uses the given span (Seurat v3 / scanpy: 0.3), on the genes ordered by log mean and log variance, so that the
    scores do not depend on the gene order. The fit passed options={'span': 0.3}, which skmisc.loess ignores (span
    0.75), and depended on the gene order at about 1e-12.
16. CellNeighborhoodTokenizer(seq_len_cell=...): explicit index-cell / neighborhood split (None: input / (k + 1)).
17. CellNeighborhoodTokenizer(aggregate_own_neighbors=True): the neighborhood is each cell's own kNN
    (connectivities @ X) instead of the cells that have it among their kNN (connectivities.T @ X).

